$BEGIN_DOCUMENTATION
Internet Archive for Theoretical Chemistry Accounts
===================================================
Title: Relativistic and nonrelativistic finite nucleus optimized double zeta
basis sets for the 4p, 5p and 6p elements.
Author: Kenneth G. Dyall
Date: 2 June 1998
This archive contains the exponents and the SCF orbital coefficients, given
with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the basis sets described in the above article. The archive is set out as
follows.

1. SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order of Z.
The large component functions are given first, followed by the small
components. Both components are labelled with the large component symmetry
type, but the functions for the small component are those obtained by
restricted kinetic balance from the large component The spin-orbit
components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for j=l+1/2.

2. SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions.
The large component functions are given first, followed by the
pseudo-large component functions. Note that both components have the same
symmetry type.

3. SCF coefficients for nonrelativistic wave functions.

4. Primitives to be added for valence flexibility. The s and p sets consists
of the function to fill the gap between the n and n-1 shells, followed by
the diffuse function optimized for the negative ion. The d set consists of
the function optimized to correlate the valence space followed by the
function optimized for the negative ion. The f set consists of the
function optimized to correlate the d shell followed by the function
optimized to polarize the d shell.

Basis set construction.
----------------------
Several different types of basis sets may be constructed from the additional
primitive functions. These are described below.

1. vdz, vcor (equivalent to cc-pvdz): add the outermost s and p primitive
from the SCF exponent set and the correlating d function to the SCF set.
(HF + 1s1p1d valence)

2. c+vdz, vcor (adding outer core relaxation): add the third outermost s and
p primitives and the outermost d primitive from the SCF exponent set to
basis set 1.
(HF + 1s1p1d valence + 1s1p1d core)

3. c+vdz, c+vcor (outer core relaxation and correlation): add the "gap"
s and p functions, the second outermost d primitive from the SCF exponent
set and the correlating f function to basis set 2.
(HF + 1s1p1d valence + 2s2p2d1f core)

4. c+vdz, c+vcor, cpol (core polarization): add the polarizing f function to
basis set 3.
(HF + 1s1p1d valence + 2s2p2d2f core)

5. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p and d functions to any of the above
basis sets.

5d-Block Double-Zeta Basis Set Archive
======================================

Title: Relativistic finite nucleus double-, triple-, and quadruple-zeta
       basis sets for the 5d elements Hf-Hg. Theor. Chem. Acc., submitted.

Author: Kenneth G. Dyall

Date: 23 Jun 2003



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients,
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the double-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  The contractions
are taken from SCF calculations on the configuration average of the d^n s^2 and
d^n+1 s^1 configurations.

The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
   of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small
   component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
   symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
   those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
   The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
   j=l+1/2.

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first,
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type.

3. Exponents of valence correlating functions.

4. Exponents of core correlating functions.


Basis set construction.
----------------------

Contracted basis sets can be constructed by adding primitive functions to the SCF
set. These basis sets are described below. Note that the term "double zeta" applies
to the s and p set, and the d set is triple zeta. This is a result of keeping a
reasonable energy balance for the basis sets. Functions are counted in increasing
exponent size, from the smallest.

1. Valence. The valence space is defined here as the 5d and 6s orbitals. To the
   SCF functions, add the 2nd s primitive, the 1st and 2nd primitives from the
   p and d sets, and the valence correlating f function.

   (HF + 1s2p2d1f valence)

2. Valence + outer core. This set adds the outer-core correlation from the 5s and 5p
   shells. To set 1, add the 4th s primitive, the 3rd p primitive and the 3rd d
   primitive.

   If linear dependence problems are encountered in the d space, delete the 5d orbital
   and optionally also delete the 6s orbital and add the 1st s primitive, or do not add
   the 3rd d primitive (on the grounds that the 5d orbital will be used to correlate
   the 5s and 5p).
   Note: excitations into the 5d shell were not considered in the determination of the
   exponents to uncontract.

   (HF + 1s2p2d1f valence + 1s1p1d outer core)

3. Valence + outer core + 4f. This set adds correlation from the 4f shell.  To set 2,
   add the 2nd and 3rd f primitives, the 4th d primitive, and the core correlating g
   function. This choice reflects only 4f correlation with a change in angular momentum
   of 1 unit. If it is necessary to uncontract an s and p primitive, add the 5th s and
   p primitives to the set.

   (HF + 1s2p2d1f valence + 1s1p1d outer core + 1d2f1g core)

4. Dipole polarization of the 5d. To any of the above basis sets, add the polarizing
   f function listed at the end of this archive.
$END_DOCUMENTATION

This file is adapted for reading with the C programme basis_set
Required are identifiers :
 - for each element as " ** Elm **" with Elm the element name (max. 3 characters),
   followed by a free format line with the atomic number.
 - for each block of basis functions an identifier $BLOCK
   followed by a free format line with the component (large 0, small 1), the l-value,
   the j-value (l-1/2=0, l+1/2=1), the number of exponents, the number of contracted
  functions. The exponents and contraction coefficients are read in free format assuming a first (dummy) integer

See for format of additional diffuse or correlating functions below.

Philosophy of the dedicated read was to preserve the original format in which this basis set was published in TCA.

Luuk Visscher, 4-6-2003.

Acknowledgements of course to Ken Dyall for providing the archive and developing the basis set !

